{
  "raw": {
    "https://jobs.keldair.com/uindy/Staff/jobs/102601/director-of-student-counseling-center": {
      "title": "Director of Student Counseling Center",
      "description": "Careers | Job Application Empty heading Director of Student Counseling Center Indianapolis, IN 46227 Posted: 10/07/2026 Deadline: Open until filled Employment: Full-time, Exempt Pay: Depends upon skills and experience Department: Student Counseling Center Experience: Mid-Level Apply Job Description The Director of the Student Counseling Center provides executive, clinical, and administrative leadership for the university’s student mental health services. This 12-month, in-person role oversees clinical operations, strategic planning, budgeting, team supervision, crisis response, and the practicum training program. The Director serves as the primary campus point-of-contact and expert on student mental health, advocating for student wellness and building collaborative partnerships across the university and the broader community. REQUIREMENTS/MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: Education: Doctoral degree (Ph.D. or Psy.D.) in Clinical or Counseling Psychology from an accredited institution. Licensure: Licensed as a Psychologist with Health Service Provider in Psychology (HSPP) endorsement in Indiana. Experience: Progressive leadership and supervisory experience in a university counseling center or multi-disciplinary clinical setting. Demonstrated expertise in crisis intervention, risk assessment, and emergency management. Experience providing clinical supervision to doctoral-level psychology trainees. Competencies: Strong administrative, budgetary, and analytical skills; commitment to ethical standards and student well-being; exceptional interpersonal and communication skills. SUPERVISORY RESPONSIBILITIES: Supervision of full-time and part-time staff in the Student Counseling Center, including the graduate interns. ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS: Executive Leadership & Operations Oversee all clinical, administrative, financial, and day-to-day operations of the Student Counseling Center. Develop, implement, and monitor departmental policies, clinical procedures, and budget allocation to maximize operational efficiency and service quality. Collect, compile, and analyze clinical utilization data, outcomes, and trends to inform service adjustments, strategy, and administrative reporting. Oversee the design and dissemination of all departmental marketing, promotional materials, and outreach initiatives. Maintain awareness of current research, clinical best practices, and ethical/legal protocols established by the American Psychological Association (APA) and state licensing boards. Clinical Supervision & Training Management Provide direct supervision, performance evaluations, and mentorship for all counseling center staff. Secure professional development resources, advocate for staff growth, and lead regular staff enrichment programs and retreats. Direct the Student Counseling Center’s Practicum Training Program in partnership with the Clinical Psychology Doctoral program. Meet regularly (at least monthly) with the Director of Clinical Training – Practicum to coordinate trainee schedules, review clinical supervision standards, and ensure seamless program alignment. Ensure Staff Psychologists/HSPP supervisors provide high-quality supervision, didactics, and mentoring to doctoral trainees. Serve on Training and Didactic program committees as needed. Clinical Service & Crisis Management When necessary, deliver direct clinical services, including individual, group, and couples therapy, comprehensive mental health assessments, and specialized alcohol and drug evaluations for policy violations. Provide immediate crisis intervention for acute student presentations (e.g., severe suicidality, homicidal ideation, psychosis, substance intoxication, sexual assault, and severe trauma). Chair the University Crisis Response Team (CRT); direct CRT initiatives, lead emergency meetings, and coordinate campus-wide outreach during acute events. Provide outreach and support to regional colleges, universities, or local agencies during major external emergency events. Serve in an \"on-call\" rotation during evenings and weekends to consult with Campus Police, Residence Life, and University Administration on emergency matters. Campus Collaboration & Community Engagement Serve as the primary campus point-of-contact and strategic resource for student mental health, consulting across academic and administrative disciplines to support total student care. Lead proactive mental health outreach and prevention efforts across the university community. Partner with academic and administrative departments to offer faculty/staff training (e.g., QPR, Mental Health First Aid) that build community capacity for early identification, crisis intervention, and student support. Collaborate closely with Health Services (nurse practitioners and office physicians) on integrated medical and psychological care plans. Partner with Residence Life to design and deliver mental health in-service training for Residence Life staff. Maintain collaborative liaison relationships with Campus Police, Residence Life, and the Vice President for Student Affairs and Student Success. Serve on university-wide committees as requested. Standard University Expectations Follow the guidance and direction of administrative leadership. Demonstrate respectful, ethical, and responsible conduct across all duties. Abide by all University policies, procedures, regulations, and employee handbook guidelines. Maintain regular, predictable, and punctual in-person attendance. Perform other duties, special projects, or committee assignments as required. Company Description Thank you for your interest in the University of Indianapolis! The University is a private, comprehensive teaching university located on 65 acres just minutes from downtown Indianapolis. With more than 700 faculty and staff, it is a place where you can become part of a close-knit campus community that emphasizes the importance of community partnerships in the future growth of the University. UIndy’s mission is to prepare its graduates for effective, responsible, and articulate membership in the complex societies in which they live and serve and for excellence and leadership in their personal and professional lives. The motto of \"Education for Service\" provides the foundation for our work to better our communities, both near and far. As University employees, we know the work we do is important to our students and our communities. To accomplish our mission, we welcome talented, civic-minded, and diverse individuals from all career levels to help maintain our high standards of excellence and quality. If you want to join our dynamic environment and experience hard work, creativity, and teamwork, we welcome your application! The University of Indianapolis is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, sex, age, color, religion, national origin, disability, citizenship status, military status, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity, or gender expression. The University of Indianapolis does not discriminate on the basis of sex in its educational programs and activities, including employment and admission as required by Title IX. Benefits Medical Insurance Vision Insurance Dental Insurance Life Insurance Tuition Discount Retirement Savings Plan - 403B Paid Vacation and Sick Time Accruals Paid Holiday time and additional University days off Employee Discounts & Services Free Parking Onsite Health & Wellness Center For more information about the benefits of being a staff member at UIndy, visit our Benefits site. Apply The University of Indianapolis is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, sex, age, color, religion, national origin, disability, citizenship status, military status, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity, or gender expression. The University of Indianapolis does not discriminate on the basis of sex in its educational programs and activities, including employment and admission as required by Title IX. Privacy Policy Please ensure Javascript is enabled for purposes of website accessibility"
    },
    "https://jobs.keldair.com/uindy/Staff/jobs/102522/director-of-principal-leadership": {
      "title": "Director of Principal Leadership",
      "description": "Careers | Job Application Empty heading Director of Principal Leadership Indianapolis, IN 46227 Posted: 10/02/2026 Deadline: Open until filled Employment: Full-time, Exempt Pay: Depends upon skills and experience Department: Center of Excellence in Leadership of Learning Experience: Mid-Level Apply Job Description The Director of Principal Leadership (DPL) provides strategic and operational oversight for the Indiana School Leaders Pipeline (ISLP), a statewide initiative funded through a federal Supporting Effective Educator Development (SEED) grant, designed to strengthen Indiana's pipeline of current and future school leaders and improve school-based leadership of evidence-based literacy and learning practices. The DPL will lead the design and implementation of professional learning, leadership development, coaching, and mentoring experiences for current principals and aspiring principals, as well as experienced principal mentors. The position will translate research into practical experiences to help school leaders strengthen instructional leadership, use data to drive continuous improvement, implement evidence-based literacy practices, and build sustainable systems within their schools. REQUIREMENTS/MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: Required Qualifications ● Master's degree in education leadership, education, school administration, organizational leadership, or a closely related field. ● Significant experience in public K–12 education, school leadership, &amp; professional learning ● Demonstrated experience working directly with principals, assistant principals, aspiring school leaders, district leaders, and/or other educational leaders. ● Knowledge of instructional leadership, school improvement, adult learning, and evidence-based approaches to improving teaching and learning. ● Demonstrated ability to facilitate professional learning and develop relationships with diverse groups of educators and stakeholders across the State of Indiana. ● Strong written, verbal, facilitation, organizational, and interpersonal communication skills. ● Experience using data to inform professional learning, coaching, school improvement, or leadership development. ● Ability to manage multiple priorities, timelines, partners, and project deliverables in a complex and collaborative environment. ● Ability and willingness to travel throughout Indiana for school visits, meetings, professional learning, and project activities. ● Familiarity with various digital communications and collaboration platforms such as Microsoft Teams, Google Meet, and Zoom. Preferred Qualifications ● An advanced degree in educational leadership, school administration, organizational leadership, or a related field. ● Building-level administrator license or comparable school leadership credential. ● Experience as a principal and / or district administrator. ● Practical experience with leadership coaching or executive coaching ● Knowledge of the Instructional Leadership Coaching (ILC) framework or competency-based leadership coaching approach. ● Knowledge of Sir John Whitmore's Coaching for Performance model, including the GROW coaching framework. ● Knowledge of evidence-based literacy instruction, the Science of Reading, and the Science of Learning. ● Experience with continuous improvement processes, data-driven instructional improvement, or school improvement planning. ● Experience collaborating with state education agencies, professional associations, higher education institutions, school districts, or other education-sector partners. SUPERVISORY RESPONSIBILITIES: None ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS: To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily. The requirements listed below are representative of the knowledge, skill, and/or ability required. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. Principal Leadership Development ● Lead the design, implementation, and continuous improvement of the ISLP's current school leaders program and Future Principals Program. ● Develop and facilitate professional learning experiences that strengthen principals' capacity to lead evidence-based literacy instruction, science of learning practices, data-driven improvement, professional learning systems, and schoolwide instructional change. ● Lead the development and implementation of virtual leadership sessions, in-person leadership academies, workshops, webinars, and other professional learning experiences. ● Translate research and evidence-based practices into practical tools, strategies, and leadership applications for school leaders. ● Support participants in developing and implementing school-level improvement strategies and action plans aligned with student learning data and state priorities. ● Incorporate participant feedback, implementation data, and evaluation findings into ongoing program improvement. Coaching and Mentoring ● Develop and maintain a coherent coaching and mentoring model for current principals participating in ISLP. ● Apply the Instructional Leadership Coaching (ILC) framework to support structured, job- embedded cycles of leadership development, goal setting, data inquiry, action, reflection, and refinement. ● Apply the principles of Sir John Whitmore's Coaching for Performance model, including the GROW approach, to promote reflection, self-awareness, goal clarity, options development, ownership, and accountability among school leaders. ● Train, support, and calibrate principal mentors in the use of evidence-based coaching and mentoring practices. ● Develop resources, tools, protocols, and structures that promote consistency and quality across mentoring relationships. ● Facilitate ongoing communication and professional learning for the mentor cohort, including opportunities to examine coaching practice, address challenges, and strengthen implementation. ● Support mentors in helping principals translate professional learning into school-based action and sustain progress over time. ● Participate in or conduct coaching and mentoring activities as appropriate to model effective practice and ensure fidelity to the project's coaching approach. Curriculum and Professional Learning Development ● Lead or contribute to the development of curriculum and materials for principal leadership training, the Leadership Academy, mentor training, and Future Principals programming. ● Integrate evidence related to the Science of Reading (SoR), Science of Learning (SoL), instructional leadership, data-driven improvement, professional learning communities, teacher development, and school improvement. ● Collaborate with subject-matter experts, school leaders, university faculty, and state education partners to identify and incorporate high-quality content and resources. ● Support the development and continuous improvement of the project&#39;s AI-focused micro-credential and the integration of responsible AI applications into leadership development and coaching. ● Ensure learning experiences are accessible, practical, relevant to participants' school contexts, and aligned with project goals. Partnership and Stakeholder Engagement ● Develop and maintain strong working relationships with IDOE, IASP, school districts, principals, university partners, and other stakeholders. ● Collaborate with partners to recruit current and aspiring principals and experienced mentors. ● Convene and facilitate meetings with project stakeholders to support alignment, communication, and implementation. ● Represent CELL and the ISLP at relevant state and professional meetings, conferences, and other external engagements. ● Build relationships with school and district leaders that support the recruitment, engagement, and success of program participants. Program Management and Evaluation ● Manage day-to-day implementation of assigned ISLP activities and ensure that project milestones and deliverables are completed on schedule. ● Coordinate project calendars, participant communications, professional learning activities, mentor activities, and other program logistics. ● Monitor participation, implementation, and other project data and use findings to inform program improvement. ● Collaborate with the external evaluator and CELL leadership to support data collection, evaluation activities, reporting, and dissemination. ● Contribute to annual and final federal project reports, research products, presentations, and the development of a toolkit to support replication of effective principal leadership development and mentoring practices. ● Maintain accurate documentation of project activities, outputs, and outcomes. ● Ensure activities are implemented consistently with grant requirements, University of Indianapolis policies, and CELL procedures. This role serves as a Title IX Responsible Employee. Yes This role serves as a Campus Security Authority. No This role requires a clean or acceptable driving record. Yes This role is considered Essential (see Extreme Weather Policy for definition). No COMPETENCIES/OTHER CHARACTERISTICS: To perform the job successfully, an individual will demonstrate the following: ● Instructional Leadership – Understands the role of school leaders in establishing coherent instructional systems and leading improvements in teaching and learning. ● Leadership Coaching – Uses structured, evidence-based coaching practices to help leaders establish meaningful goals, examine current reality, identify options, take action, monitor progress, and reflect on results. ● Facilitation and Adult Learning – Designs and facilitates learning experiences that are relevant, engaging, practical, and responsive to adult learners. ● Data-Informed Improvement – Uses multiple sources of quantitative and qualitative data to identify needs, monitor implementation, assess progress, and inform leadership decisions. ● Relationship Building – Establishes trust and productive working relationships with principals, mentors, district leaders, state agency representatives, university colleagues, and other stakeholders. ● Strategic Thinking – Connects individual professional learning and coaching activities to broader school, district, state, and project goals. ● Communication – Communicates clearly and effectively with diverse audiences through written, verbal, virtual, and in-person formats. ● Project Management – Effectively manages competing priorities, timelines, deliverables, resources, and stakeholder expectations. ● Continuous Improvement – Uses feedback, evidence, reflection, and evaluation findings to strengthen programs and professional practice. Company Description Thank you for your interest in the University of Indianapolis! The University is a private, comprehensive teaching university located on 65 acres just minutes from downtown Indianapolis. With more than 700 faculty and staff, it is a place where you can become part of a close-knit campus community that emphasizes the importance of community partnerships in the future growth of the University. UIndy’s mission is to prepare its graduates for effective, responsible, and articulate membership in the complex societies in which they live and serve and for excellence and leadership in their personal and professional lives. The motto of \"Education for Service\" provides the foundation for our work to better our communities, both near and far. As University employees, we know the work we do is important to our students and our communities. To accomplish our mission, we welcome talented, civic-minded, and diverse individuals from all career levels to help maintain our high standards of excellence and quality. If you want to join our dynamic environment and experience hard work, creativity, and teamwork, we welcome your application! The University of Indianapolis is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, sex, age, color, religion, national origin, disability, citizenship status, military status, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity, or gender expression. The University of Indianapolis does not discriminate on the basis of sex in its educational programs and activities, including employment and admission as required by Title IX. Benefits Medical Insurance Vision Insurance Dental Insurance Life Insurance Tuition Discount Retirement Savings Plan - 403B Paid Vacation and Sick Time Accruals Paid Holiday time and additional University days off Employee Discounts & Services Free Parking Onsite Health & Wellness Center For more information about the benefits of being a staff member at UIndy, visit our Benefits site. Apply The University of Indianapolis is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, sex, age, color, religion, national origin, disability, citizenship status, military status, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity, or gender expression. The University of Indianapolis does not discriminate on the basis of sex in its educational programs and activities, including employment and admission as required by Title IX. Privacy Policy Please ensure Javascript is enabled for purposes of website accessibility"
    },
    "https://jobs.keldair.com/uindy/Staff/jobs/102424/police-officer": {
      "title": "Police Officer",
      "description": "Careers | Job Application Empty heading Police Officer Indianapolis, IN 46227 Posted: 09/29/2026 Deadline: Open until filled Employment: Full-time, Non-exempt Pay: Depends upon skills and experience Department: University Police Department Experience: Mid-Level Apply Job Description Police Officer duties focus on protecting people and property at the University of Indianapolis and surrounding areas. Officers patrol an established jurisdiction where they respond to calls, enforce laws, make arrests, issue citations, and testify in court. Officers are responsible for making traffic stops and ensuring traffic safety in and around the university. The officer’s time is divided between patrolling, writing reports, conducting educational programs, and continued training. REQUIREMENTS/MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: Experience: N/A Education: High School graduate or equivalent Licenses/Certifications/Credentials: A valid driver’s license that meets the University’s driving policy, must be able to successfully pass the minimum standards to attend the ILEA academy. ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS: To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily. The requirements listed below are representative of the knowledge, skill, and/or ability required. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. Protects the lives of students, faculty, staff, and campus visitors, and also protects property and facilities through enforcement of laws and regulations. Responds to calls for police services. Analyzes and reacts to emergency situations quickly, calmly, and correctly to adapt an effective course of action, giving due regard to surrounding hazards and circumstances. Proactively patrols assigned areas. Enforces laws and traffic regulations, conducts interviews, apprehends violators, and makes arrests. Conducts and assists in criminal and traffic investigations. Prepares written reports and field notes of investigations and patrol activities. Testifies in court. Exercises judgment in determining when to use force and to what degree. Communicates with the campus community, co-workers, subordinates, or others to provide directions and training, explain departmental policies and procedures, laws, ordinances, codes, etc., resolve complaints and personnel matters, and gather and disseminate information. Ensures operation of equipment by practicing responsible use, completing preventive maintenance requirements, following manufacturer’s instructions, troubleshooting malfunctions, notifying supervisor of needed repairs, and evaluating new equipment and techniques. Maintains compliance with law, codes, university policy, and department’s general orders. Shares information and works with other law enforcement agencies as directed and appropriate. Provides general information and assistance to the public. Follows the guidance and direction of supervisor(s) Performs employee responsibilities as detailed in the Staff Handbook Abide by the University’s policies, procedures, and rules. Demonstrates respectful, ethical, responsible behavior. Demonstrates regular and predictable attendance and punctuality. Other duties as required or assigned This role serves as a Title IX Responsible Employee. Yes This role serves as a Campus Security Authority. Yes This role requires a clean or acceptable driving record. Yes This role is considered Essential (see Extreme Weather Policy for definition). Yes COMPETENCIES/OTHER CHARACTERISTICS: To perform the job successfully, an individual will demonstrate the following: Strong moral and ethical character Service-oriented approach and positive attitude Ability to withstand emotional, physical, and mentally taxing experiences Ability to use sound judgment and to problem solve Ability to understand the perspectives of many people and have a willingness to help the community Flexibility and the ability to adapt to an ever-changing work environment Ability to exercise discretion Excellent verbal, written, and interpersonal communication skills Ability to remain calm in emergencies, organized, and thorough Ability to implement tactics and strategic procedure Basic computer skills PHYSICAL DEMANDS: The physical demands described here are representative of those that must be met by an employee to successfully perform the essential functions of this job. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. WORK-PLACE ACTIVITIES* * Frequencies based on DOT (Dictionary of Occupational Titles) standard frequencies Activity Never 0% of the day 0 per day 0 Occasional 1-33% of day 1-66 per day 1 per 15 min. Frequent 34-66% of day 67-200 per day 1 per 5 min. Constant 67-100% of day 200 plus per day 1 per 30 sec. Standing X Sitting X Walking X Bending X Forward X Sideways X Climbing X Grasping X Pushing (Carts, tables, etc) X Pulling (Carts, wheelchairs, etc) X Twisting at waist X Work above shoulders X Repetitive arm movement X Reaching X Overhead: X Forward: X Side: X Rear: X Squatting X Kneeling X Crawling X Wrist & Forearm X Pronation / Supination X Other: Other: May drive between worksites X Lifting and Carrying Never Occasional Frequent Constant Under 10 lbs X 10-25 lbs X 25-40 lbs X 40-55 lbs X 55-70 lbs X 70 + X Company Description Thank you for your interest in the University of Indianapolis! The University is a private, comprehensive teaching university located on 65 acres just minutes from downtown Indianapolis. With more than 700 faculty and staff, it is a place where you can become part of a close-knit campus community that emphasizes the importance of community partnerships in the future growth of the University. UIndy’s mission is to prepare its graduates for effective, responsible, and articulate membership in the complex societies in which they live and serve and for excellence and leadership in their personal and professional lives. The motto of \"Education for Service\" provides the foundation for our work to better our communities, both near and far. As University employees, we know the work we do is important to our students and our communities. To accomplish our mission, we welcome talented, civic-minded, and diverse individuals from all career levels to help maintain our high standards of excellence and quality. If you want to join our dynamic environment and experience hard work, creativity, and teamwork, we welcome your application! The University of Indianapolis is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, sex, age, color, religion, national origin, disability, citizenship status, military status, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity, or gender expression. The University of Indianapolis does not discriminate on the basis of sex in its educational programs and activities, including employment and admission as required by Title IX. Benefits Medical Insurance Vision Insurance Dental Insurance Life Insurance Tuition Discount Retirement Savings Plan - 403B Paid Vacation and Sick Time Accruals Paid Holiday time and additional University days off Employee Discounts & Services Free Parking Onsite Health & Wellness Center For more information about the benefits of being a staff member at UIndy, visit our Benefits site. Apply The University of Indianapolis is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, sex, age, color, religion, national origin, disability, citizenship status, military status, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity, or gender expression. The University of Indianapolis does not discriminate on the basis of sex in its educational programs and activities, including employment and admission as required by Title IX. Privacy Policy Please ensure Javascript is enabled for purpose