    "https://jobs.keldair.com/uindy/Staff/jobs/101240/residence-life-coordinator": {
      "title": "Residence Life Coordinator",
      "description": "Careers | Job Application Empty heading Residence Life Coordinator Indianapolis, IN 46227 Posted: 10/04/2026 Deadline: Open until filled Employment: Full-time, Exempt Pay: Depends upon skills and experience Department: Residence Life Experience: Intermediate Apply Job Description The Residence Life Coordinator (RLC) is a professional member of the Office of Residence Life and is responsible for the overall administration of their assigned residential areas, student staff, and community development. The RLC will also be assigned to a functional area and departmental responsibilities to enhance student engagement and fulfill the mission of the Division of Student Affairs and Student Success. This position is a full-time 12-month position with a live-on requirement that includes a partially furnished apartment and a meal plan with the expectation of using University Dining options to promote community development for staff and students and, when necessary, assist with policy enforcement. REQUIREMENTS/MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: Required: Experience working with undergraduate and graduate students. Supervisory experience or previous experience working in university settings. Skilled in excellent professional interpersonal, verbal, and written communication skills, including the ability to work effectively with all individuals. Preferred: Previous experience living or working in residence life or student leadership roles (1–3 years). Organizing multistep or long-term projects (e.g., Student Staff Training, Student Staff Recruitment) Education: Bachelor’s degree from an accredited institution required. Master’s degree in Higher Education Administration, Student Affairs, Counseling, Education, or related field is preferred. SUPERVISORY RESPONSIBILITIES: Supervision of undergraduate or graduate paraprofessional student staff, such as Resident Assistants, Apartment Community Assistants, Graduate Assistants, desk workers, and other assigned staff. ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS: Supervision: Provide direct supervision to Resident Assistants (RAs) or Apartment Community Assistants (ACAs) and/or Graduate Assistants. Participate in the recruitment, selection, training, development, and evaluation of undergraduate and graduate student staff. Maintain weekly staff meetings and recurring biweekly individual meetings for ongoing development. Set clear performance expectations and hold staff accountable to departmental and university processes, procedures, and policies. Provide indirect supervision to additional student staff members or desk assistants working in assigned communities. Promote a positive team environment by identifying, addressing, and resolving problems within the team. Community Development: Facilitate the implementation of the Residence Life Engagement Model within assigned areas to develop a sense of belonging within the residential communities. Oversee Living Learning Communities (LLCs) within assigned areas, and partner with faculty liaisons to promote student learning and development. Maintain a consistent level of visibility and interact with students on a regular basis. Support students by addressing their needs and concerns to overcome challenges and achieve success. Connect students with campus resources to promote personal, academic, and social growth. Collaborate with campus partners on initiatives to create safety nets for students' well-being. Building Management and Administration: Maintain accurate administrative records: building occupancy, key inventories, student conduct management, budget accounts, and other housing-related processes. Oversee desk operations and staff scheduling. Monitor, maintain, and approve payroll for assigned staff members. Collaborate with University Facilities, Housekeeping, or Apartment Management to ensure communities are well-maintained. Assist in move-in, break housing, and move-out processes each semester. Utilize departmental databases and online systems for management of day-to-day operations, including but not limited to Housing.Cloud, Maxient, Success Slate, Google Drive Workspaces, etc. Student Conduct and Crisis Management: Share, communicate, and enforce all residence hall, apartment, and student conduct policies. Serve as a hearing officer and adjudicator of assigned university conduct cases. Serve in an on-call rotation with other Professional Staff as the primary contact person to assist in emergency or crisis situations. On-call responsibilities are for the entire residential campus. (Includes university break periods and holidays) Serve on Title IX or University Conduct Boards as needed. Departmental Responsibilities: Lead and oversee a functional area initiative related to Student Staff Training, Living Learning Communities, Student Staff Employment, Residence Life Engagement Model, or other identified area. Assist other professional staff members with their functional areas as requested. Assist with departmental and divisional programmatic initiatives, which may require collaboration with other campus offices. Fulfill summer responsibilities that may include working with Conferences and Event Services, Admissions, Orientation, Summer School, Student Staff Training, special projects, etc. Participate in assessment and retention efforts by contributing feedback, supporting data collection, and collaborating on strategies to enhance student success and persistence. Participate in weekly meetings with the Residence Life Professional Staff Team. Opportunities to serve on additional University Committees as a Residence Life representative. Additional Responsibilities: Follow the guidance and direction of supervisor(s). Demonstrate respectful, ethical, and responsible behavior. Abide by the University’s policies, procedures, and rules. Perform employee responsibilities as detailed in employee handbooks and policies. Demonstrate regular and predictable attendance and punctuality. Other duties, including special projects, as required or assigned. Company Description Thank you for your interest in the University of Indianapolis! The University is a private, liberal arts university located on 65 acres just minutes from downtown Indianapolis. With more than 700 faculty and staff, it is a place where you can become part of a close-knit campus community that emphasizes the importance of community partnerships in the future growth of the University. UIndy’s mission is to prepare its graduates for effective, responsible, and articulate membership in the complex societies in which they live and serve and for excellence and leadership in their personal and professional lives. The motto of \"Education for Service\" provides the foundation for our work to better our communities, both near and far. As University employees, we know the work we do is important to our students and our communities. To accomplish our mission, we welcome talented, civic-minded, and diverse individuals from all career levels to help maintain our high standards of excellence and quality. If you want to join our dynamic environment and experience hard work, creativity, and teamwork, we welcome your application! The University of Indianapolis is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, sex, age, color, religion, national origin, disability, citizenship status, military status, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity, or gender expression. The University of Indianapolis does not discriminate on the basis of sex in its educational programs and activities, including employment and admission as required by Title IX. Benefits Medical Insurance Vision Insurance Dental Insurance Life Insurance Tuition Discount Retirement Savings Plan - 403B Paid Vacation and Sick Time Accruals Paid Holiday time and additional University days off Employee Discounts & Services Free Parking Onsite Health & Wellness Center For more information about the benefits of being a staff member at UIndy, visit our Benefits site. Apply The University of Indianapolis is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, sex, age, color, religion, national origin, disability, citizenship status, military status, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity, or gender expression. The University of Indianapolis does not discriminate on the basis of sex in its educational programs and activities, including employment and admission as required by Title IX. Privacy Policy Please ensure Javascript is enabled for purposes of website accessibility"
    },
    "https://jobs.keldair.com/uindy/Staff/jobs/101239/painter": {
      "title": "Painter",
      "description": "Careers | Job Application Empty heading Painter Indianapolis, IN 46227 Posted: 10/04/2026 Deadline: Open until filled Employment: Full-time, Non-exempt Pay: $18.00 - $20.00 per Hour Department: Facilities Management Experience: Intermediate Apply Job Description The University of Indianapolis is looking for a skilled Facilities Painter to help keep our campus looking its best. This is a hands-on painting and finishing role with room to take real ownership of how the campus presents itself — from residence halls and academic buildings to offices and common spaces across our grounds. If you take pride in clean lines, a well-prepped surface, and a finished space that looks sharp, this is a place where that work is seen and valued. This is a full-time, benefited position with a stable weekday schedule and one of the strongest benefits packages you'll find in the trades — including free tuition for you and your family. This is an essential staff position. The facilities department maintains a shared on-call emergency rotation across the maintenance staff, and this role takes part in that rotation. Essential staff may be asked to report to work when the university is otherwise closed or during declared inclement-weather emergencies. We're upfront about this because it's part of taking care of the campus and the people in it — but it's a shared responsibility, not one that falls on any single person. REQUIREMENTS/QUALIFICATIONS: Experience — Three years of painting experience, with a solid working knowledge of general building maintenance. Education — High school diploma or GED. Physical — Comfortable working at heights and in tight spaces, able to safely operate lifts to reach high areas, and able to lift up to 55 pounds. Safety — Willingness to complete OSHA safety training, use required PPE, and follow all Physical Plant safety standards. Driving — A clean or otherwise acceptable driving record. Approach — Self-directed and resourceful, with a customer-service mindset and a genuine eye for making a space look its best. If you have a strong foundation in painting and finishing and take pride in your work, we're glad to help you round out the general-maintenance side of the role. A good painter who cares about the craft is what we're really after. ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS: Your core work is painting and finishing across a large, active campus — surface prep, priming, and finish-coating drywall, metal door and window frames, and other surfaces as needed, along with drywall patching and wall touch-up to make those finishes look right. Interior and exterior caulking is a regular part of the job, as is patching and hanging vinyl wall coverings. You'll measure and estimate square footage and lineal footage to order the correct paint and materials, and keep the paint shop's supplies and inventory organized and stocked. Beyond individual work orders, you'll have a hand in the bigger picture — helping build and maintain a campus-wide painting plan that prioritizes needs so the university always puts its best face forward. You'll respond to painting work order requests as they come in, and because you'll be moving all over campus, you'll serve as another set of eyes for the facilities team, flagging other maintenance needs so we can stay ahead of them. No one wants to paint the same wall types day after day with nothing to break it up, and this role has natural variety built in. You will regularly lend a hand to the carpentry team on the kinds of jobs that get you out of the paint shop for a while — hanging pictures, TVs, and whiteboards, helping with office and furniture moves, and assisting with storefront and glass work. It is a welcome change of pace that keeps the days varied while the carpenters get an extra set of capable hands. Like everyone in facilities, you'll also pitch in on general maintenance and cross-departmental work when the situation calls for it — event setups, snow removal, and emergency response are shared efforts across the team. General Employee Expectations: Follow the guidance and direction of supervisor(s). Demonstrate respectful, ethical, responsible behavior. Abide by the University’s policies, procedures, and rules. Perform employee responsibilities as detailed in employee handbooks and policies. Demonstrate regular and predictable attendance and punctuality. Other duties, including special projects, as required or assigned. Company Description Thank you for your interest in the University of Indianapolis! The University is a private, liberal arts university located on 65 acres just minutes from downtown Indianapolis. With more than 700 faculty and staff, it is a place where you can become part of a close-knit campus community that emphasizes the importance of community partnerships in the future growth of the University. UIndy’s mission is to prepare its graduates for effective, responsible, and articulate membership in the complex societies in which they live and serve and for excellence and leadership in their personal and professional lives. The motto of \"Education for Service\" provides the foundation for our work to better our communities, both near and far. As University employees, we know the work we do is important to our students and our communities. To accomplish our mission, we welcome talented, civic-minded, and diverse individuals from all career levels to help maintain our high standards of excellence and quality. If you want to join our dynamic environment and experience hard work, creativity, and teamwork, we welcome your application! The University of Indianapolis is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, sex, age, color, religion, national origin, disability, citizenship status, military status, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity, or gender expression. The University of Indianapolis does not discriminate on the basis of sex in its educational programs and activities, including employment and admission as required by Title IX. Benefits Medical Insurance Vision Insurance Dental Insurance Life Insurance Tuition Discount Retirement Savings Plan - 403B Paid Vacation and Sick Time Accruals Paid Holiday time and additional University days off Employee Discounts & Services Free Parking Onsite Health & Wellness Center For more information about the benefits of being a staff member at UIndy, visit our Benefits site. Apply The University of Indianapolis is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, sex, age, color, religion, national origin, disability, citizenship status, military status, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity, or gender expression. The University of Indianapolis does not discriminate on the basis of sex in its educational programs and activities, including employment and admission as required by Title IX. Privacy Policy Please ensure Javascript is enabled for purposes of website accessibility"
    },
    "https://jobs.keldair.com/uindy/Staff/jobs/101235/lead-custodian-supervisor": {
      "title": "Lead Custodian Supervisor",
      "description": "Careers | Job Application Empty heading Lead Custodian Supervisor Indianapolis, IN 46227 Posted: 10/04/2026 Deadline: Open until filled Employment: Full-time, Non-exempt Pay: Depends upon skills and experience Department: Facilities Management Experience: Intermediate Apply Job Description Oversee daily cleaning responsibilities of custodians. REQUIREMENTS/MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: Experience: At least 3 years in the custodial field. Requires intermediate knowledge of cleaning terms and safety procedures related to hazardous chemicals. Education: High School Diploma/GED SUPERVISORY RESPONSIBILITIES: The Supervisor will be responsible for training and overseeing of the daily custodian operations in the academic buildings and dorms. ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS: Oversee the day-to-day operations of 1st & 3rd shift Custodians. Regular communication with the manager is required, including at least two (2) daily check-ins to review progress, address challenges, communicate needs or concerns, or confirm next steps for tasks or projects. Provide guidance, training and mentorship to employees and address employee issues. Inspect, evaluate and provide feedback to team members. Ensure all employees are adhering to company policies, industry regulations and safety guidelines. Participate in leadership and staff meetings to communicate objectives and share updates. Approve attendance and Web Time Entry. Assign relief custodians to fill in for absentee workers. Follow the guidance and direction of the Manager. Other duties, including special projects, as required or assigned. Company Description Thank you for your interest in the University of Indianapolis! The University is a private, liberal arts university located on 65 acres just minutes from downtown Indianapolis. With more than 700 faculty and staff, it is a place where you can become part of a close-knit campus community that emphasizes the importance of community partnerships in the future growth of the Univer